No, that's false logic - you can't break down the populations arbitrarily 
like that. You need to take the full set of successful outcomes, which 
includes all the paths in which you would have chosen not to keep the 
conduct. You also need to attribute probabilities to each outcome - for 
example giving the character a chanced of the "best" move and a chance of a 
random move on each turn - to weight the possible paths.

>> But you can look at all the games played as a sample of all possible 
>> games[1]. In that case, you need to look at proportions - I expect
>> that a greater proportion of all Guardian Heroes games have ended
>> successfully than Nethack games; if true, this would make it likely
>> that nethack is harder.
> 
> It is almost certainly true.  Nethack is certainly the harder game.
> 
> Although if we use the population test, shouldn't games with analog
> real-time controls tend to be harder?  If you just fed such games
> random inputs, I bet you would have an even smaller success rate than
> doing so with nethack.  Probably by a large margin.

No modern PC or games console I'm aware of has true analog controls - 
merely more levels of discrete input. But you also need to have some kind 
of model of player behaviour - totally random behaviour isn't really a good 
model - you want sensible moves to be more likely in some way, weighting 
the possible outcomes.

Choosing a good model doesn't matter so much if you are comparing similar 
things - e.g. nethack vanilla ascensions to conduct ascensions - but 
matters a lot if you're comparing very different things.


